In a sense, likelihood works backwards from probability: given B, we use the conditional probability P(A | B) to réason about A, and, given A, we use the likelihood function P(A | B) to réason about B.
This mode of réasoning is formalized in &lt;a href="Bayes%27%20theorem"&gt;Bayes' theorem&lt;/a&gt;; 
