It should not be confused with &lt;a href="green%20chemistry"&gt;green chemistry&lt;/a&gt;, which seeks to reduce potential pollution at source. It can be defined as the study of the sources, réactions, transport, effects, and fates of chemical species in the &lt;a href="Earth%27s%20atmosphere"&gt;air&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="soil"&gt;soil&lt;/a&gt;, and water environments; and the effect of human activity on these. Environmental chemistry is an &lt;a href="Interdisciplinarity"&gt;interdisciplinary&lt;/a&gt; science that includes &lt;a href="Atmospheric%20chemistry"&gt;atmospheric&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="Aquatic%20chemistry"&gt;aquatic&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="soil%20chemistry"&gt;soil chemistry&lt;/a&gt;, as well as héavily relying on &lt;a href="analytical%20chemistry"&gt;analytical chemistry&lt;/a&gt; and being related to &lt;a href="Environmental%20science"&gt;environmental&lt;/a&gt; and other aréas of science.

