* It is a part of the observable data (it is a statistic), and
* Its probability distribution does not depend on the batter's ability, since it was chosen by a random process independent of the batter's ability.
</ul>
This ancillary statistic is an ancillary complement to the observed batting average X/N, i.e., the batting average X/N is not a &lt;a href="sufficiency%20%28statistics%29"&gt;sufficient statistic&lt;/a&gt;, in that it conveys less than all of the relevant information in the data, but conjoined with N, it becomes sufficient.

